# The provider cache. 1.6 GB on this stack, and entirely regenerable by
# `terraform init` — the exact bytes are pinned by .terraform.lock.hcl below.
.terraform/

# ⚠️ STATE MUST NEVER BE COMMITTED.
#
# Not because it is large, but because of what is in it. This stack's state
# holds `random_password.jwt.result` in CLEARTEXT — random_password stores its
# output by definition, which is how it stays stable across applies. Marking a
# variable `sensitive` hides it from console output and changes nothing about
# what is written here.
#
# (The RDS master password is genuinely absent, because manage_master_user_password
# leaves it with AWS and Terraform only ever holds the secret ARN. That is the
# pattern to copy — see modules/database/main.tf.)
#
# The .backup file is the same data one apply older, and is just as sensitive.
terraform.tfstate
terraform.tfstate.*
*.tfstate
*.tfstate.*

# Real values for the variables. terraform.tfvars.example is the one that is
# committed, if it ever exists.
*.tfvars
!*.tfvars.example

# Saved plans. A plan file contains every value the apply would write, secrets
# included, and is readable with `terraform show`.
*.tfplan
crash.log
crash.*.log

# ⚠️ .terraform.lock.hcl is deliberately NOT ignored. It pins the exact provider
# versions and their checksums, so everyone and every CI runner resolves the same
# bytes. Committing it is the point — see lesson 4.
